# wy/wyrm/compiler/functions.wy - epic 10 M4
#
# Wyrm port of pypoc/wypoc/compiler_bc/functions.py: parameter/capture
# layout, a function's two-pass prologue, its body and epilogue, a nested
# `fn`/lambda/`defer` closure, and the table-index reservation that makes a
# self-referential or top-level-published function possible before its own
# body is compiled.
#
# A function body is compiled into its own `FnContext` buffer and laid out
# after module init (M6's `module.wy`), so its code offset is patched in at
# assembly time. Its table index, though, is reserved *before* the body is
# compiled - `compile_callable` below calls `ModuleImage.add_function` first
# and compiles the body second, exactly as functions.py does, for the same
# two reasons: module init needs the index for the `closure` that publishes
# a top-level function, and a self-referential body needs it too.
#
# Nested functions are where the interesting work is. A nested `fn`, a
# lambda and a `defer` block are all compiled the same way: work out what the
# body reads from the enclosing frame (its captures), compile it as an
# ordinary function whose P frame carries those captures after its
# parameters, then emit a `closure` in the enclosing frame that copies them
# in. A captured variable that anyone assigns is held in a cell instead, so
# the copy shares a box rather than a value (spec 8.3) - the PAIR-chain
# convention `context.wy`'s header and `analysis.wy`'s `cell_names` already
# document: a one-element pair list is the box, `getidx`/`setidx` index 0
# read and write it, and `plist ... f=1` builds one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deviations from functions.py, each forced by the port
#
# 1. Node field access, not attribute access. `node.class_target`,
#    `node.params`, `node.body`, `node.name`, `node.pos` become
#    `_field(node, n)` against wy/wyrm's node-kind table (`ast.wy`):
#    `fn_def`/`co_def` are `(name, dispatch, params, ret_type, body)`
#    (fields 1-5); a nested-fn/lambda/`co_lambda` body's params/body are
#    read through `analysis.wy`'s `boundary_params`/`boundary_body`, which
#    already knows each kind's field layout (including `co_lambda`'s
#    `co_params` wrapper) - reused here rather than re-deriving it.
#    `defer`'s field 1 is its `on <type>` constraint (nil for a plain
#    `defer:`); there is no `node.pos` at all (no milestone has position
#    tracking yet, per `handlers.wy`/`statements.wy`'s own notes), so every
#    `pos` parameter below is threaded through as `nil` and never read.
#
# 2. No exceptions, so no `CompileError` "started emitting, then reraise or
#    stub" flow. functions.py's `_compile_or_stub` runs
#    `_allocate_slots`/`_prologue`/`_body_and_epilogue`/`patch_labels`
#    inside a `try`, catching `CompileError` and either reraising (when
#    `module.stub_unlowered` is false) or replacing what was emitted with a
#    `trap` stub. This port gets the identical state machine from ordinary
#    `is error` checks: `_try_compile` runs the same four steps, stopping at
#    the first `error`, and `_compile_or_stub` decides whether to propagate
#    that `error` outward (aborting the whole `compile_callable` the way an
#    uncaught Python exception would) or swallow it into a stub, exactly
#    mirroring the try/except's two outcomes.
#
# 3. `frame.captures`/`frame.cells` are `list`s here, not Python-dict-as-set /
#    `set`. `context.wy`'s `FnContext.captures` is a `dict` (name -> P
#    register, for `lookup`), so `set(frame.captures)` - which Python's
#    `_allocate_slots`/`_prologue` lean on for the capture *names* - has no
#    direct wy equivalent (no `dict.keys()` in this codebase; see
#    `context.wy`'s own header for the general "no object-identity/no dict-
#    keys" theme). So `_allocate_slots`/`_prologue`/`_try_compile` below
#    thread the `captures` *name list* through as an explicit parameter
#    instead of re-deriving it from `frame.captures`, alongside `params`
#    (already an explicit name list in pypoc too). `frame.cells` is already a
#    `list` in this port (`analysis.wy`'s `cell_names`/`intersect` build one
#    directly, deliberately not a set - see that file's header), so nothing
#    changes there.
#
# 4. No `sorted()`/dict-as-set difference builtin. `_prologue`'s
#    `sorted(frame.cells - set(frame.captures))` becomes `_cells_to_seed` (a
#    manual `list` difference by membership) piped through `_sort_names` (a
#    small insertion sort over `str(sym)` spellings - the cell set is a
#    handful of names per frame, so an O(n^2) sort costs nothing).
#
# 5. `constant_value`/`NOT_CONSTANT` are not imported from expressions.wy -
#    they are not ported there yet (M2's scope stopped short of them; grep
#    confirms). Rather than touch expressions.wy beyond the one hook this
#    milestone is scoped to add, `_constant_value` is a small local copy
#    here, in this port's own idiom: it returns the literal's value, or an
#    `error` standing in for pypoc's `NOT_CONSTANT` sentinel (this port has
#    no singleton-identity primitive to build a sentinel object from, and
#    "fallible op returns error" is what every other file in this package
#    already does for exactly this shape of question).
#
# 6. `make_fn_param`, not image.py's `Param` dataclass or image.wy's `n`/`d`
#    dict shape. `context.wy`'s `ModuleImage` (note 1 in its own header) is
#    the mutable pool this file writes into, and it already defines
#    `make_fn_param(name: str, default=nil)`; M6's `module.wy` is what turns
#    those into `image.wy`'s wire-shaped `{"n":.., "d":..}` dicts, not this
#    file's job.
#
# 7. Closing the cycle-breaking hooks M2/M3 left open, per the epic's
#    instructions - and adding one more of the same shape. This file needs
#    `compile_body`/`store_reg` from statements.wy, so it imports
#    statements.wy directly; that means statements.wy cannot import this
#    file back (wy/wyrm's import graph must stay acyclic,
#    pypoc/doc/addendum.md), so `_nested_fn`/`_defer` cannot call
#    `compile_nested_fn`/`compile_defer` by importing them. All three
#    call-ins - `expressions.wy`'s `_lambda`, `statements.wy`'s
#    `_nested_fn` and `_defer` - are one-shot hooks instead
#    (`set_closure_compiler`, added to expressions.wy this milestone
#    mirroring its own `set_body_compiler`; `set_nested_fn_compiler`/
#    `set_defer_compiler`, added to statements.wy this milestone the same
#    way), installed once at this file's own bottom. Nothing else in either
#    file changed.

import std::pairs::*
import wyrm::opcodes::*
import wyrm::compiler::context::*
import wyrm::compiler::analysis::*
import wyrm::compiler::expressions::*
import wyrm::compiler::statements::*

# image.py's function-table flag bits (doc/wyc-format.md 8.4: bit 0
# coroutine, bit 1 message/dispatch - not set by this file, M5's job - bit 2
# `*args`, bit 3 `**kwargs`). Not exported by context.wy/image.wy (neither
# ports image.py's flag constants), so frozen here the same way
# context.wy's TABLE_LIMIT/MAX_LOCALS are.
FN_COROUTINE := 1
FN_VARARGS := 4
FN_KWARGS := 8

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# small local helpers - copied rather than imported; see analysis.wy's
# header for why (no cross-module access to a leading-underscore name, and
# std::pairs' `list_tail`/`field` walk with a `for ... in range(...)`, which
# never iterates on this repo's C VM).

fn _tail(node, count: int):
    cur := node
    i := 0
    while i < count:
        if not (cur is pair):
            return error("index out of range")
        cur = cdr(cur)
        i = i + 1
    return cur

fn _field(node, index: int):
    rest := _tail(node, index)
    if rest is error:
        return rest
    return car(rest)

fn _copy_list(items: list) -> list:
    out := []
    for item in items:
        out ! append(item)
    return out

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# emit helpers - pack (or pack_pairable) THEN emit, checking the error each
# can return in one place. Copied from expressions.wy/statements.wy for the
# same reason `_field`/`_tail` are.

fn _emit_pack(frame: FnContext, op, f: int = 0, a0: int = 0, a1: int = 0, a2: int = 0, w1: int | nil = nil) -> nil | error:
    words := pack(op, f=f, a0=a0, a1=a1, a2=a2, w1=w1)
    if words is error:
        return words
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

fn _emit_pairable(frame: FnContext, op, primary: int = 0, secondary: int = 0) -> nil | error:
    words := pack_pairable(op, primary, secondary)
    if words is error:
        return words
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the entry point: a `fn`/`co` definition

# Compile a `fn`/`co` definition, returning `[function index, capture
# names]`. `enclosing` is the frame this definition sits in, if any; the
# names its body reads from that frame become its captures, in
# first-encounter order (analysis.wy's `free_names`).
fn compile_function(node, module, name = nil, flags: int = 0, enclosing = nil, tolerant: bool = false) -> list | error:
    dispatch_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    if not (dispatch_node is nil):
        return error("the bytecode compiler does not support dispatch on a class target (`frame [T] name`) yet")
    f := flags
    if node_kind(node) == 'co_def:
        f = f | FN_COROUTINE
    fn_name := name
    if fn_name is nil:
        fn_name = str((_field(node, 1) catch nil))
    # A `co_def` carries its parameters inside a `co_params` node (its
    # first field is the send type); boundary_params unwraps both shapes.
    params := boundary_params(node)
    body := (_field(node, 5) catch nil)
    return compile_callable(module, fn_name, params, body, nil, f, enclosing, [], nil, tolerant)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# parameters

# `[names, entries, flags]` for a parameter list. `entries` is what the
# functions table records - each parameter's name and, when it has one, the
# static-pool index of its default. A `*args`/`**kwargs` parameter is an
# ordinary P slot that arrives pre-collected; only the flag says so
# (spec 4.6).
fn param_spec(params, module = nil) -> list | error:
    names := []
    entries := []
    flags := 0
    seen_collector := false
    p := params
    while p is pair:
        param := car(p)
        kind := node_kind(param)
        if kind == 'var_positional:
            flags = flags | FN_VARARGS
            seen_collector = true
            name := (_field(param, 1) catch nil)
            names ! append(name)
            entries ! append(make_fn_param(str(name), nil))
        elif kind == 'var_keyword:
            flags = flags | FN_KWARGS
            seen_collector = true
            name2 := (_field(param, 1) catch nil)
            names ! append(name2)
            entries ! append(make_fn_param(str(name2), nil))
        else:
            name3 := (_field(param, 1) catch nil)
            if seen_collector:
                return error("parameter '" + str(name3) + "' follows a collecting parameter")
            default_node := (_field(param, 3) catch nil)
            default := nil
            if not (default_node is nil):
                value := _constant_value(default_node)
                if value is error:
                    return error("parameter '" + str(name3) + "': a default must be a constant")
                if module is nil:
                    return error("parameter '" + str(name3) + "': a default must be a constant")
                idx := (module.image) ! add_static(value)
                if idx is error:
                    return idx
                default = idx
            names ! append(name3)
            entries ! append(make_fn_param(str(name3), default))
        p = cdr(p)
    return [names, entries, flags]

fn param_names(params, module = nil) -> list | error:
    spec := param_spec(params, module)
    if spec is error:
        return spec
    return spec[0]

# The Python value of a literal, or an `error` standing in for
# expressions.py's `NOT_CONSTANT` sentinel - see deviation 5. Used only
# where the format can hold a constant: a parameter default.
fn _constant_value(node):
    if node is sym:
        if node == 'nil:
            return nil
        if node == 'true:
            return true
        if node == 'false:
            return false
        return error("not a constant")
    kind := node_kind(node)
    if (kind == 'str) or (kind == 'int) or (kind == 'float) or (kind == 'char):
        return (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    return error("not a constant")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# captures and cells (spec 8.3) - the epic's highest-risk area

# The free names of `body` that the enclosing frame can actually hand over.
# Anything else - a module global, a builtin, an external name - is reached
# the same way it would be from any other scope, not captured.
fn _captures(names: list, body, enclosing) -> list:
    if enclosing is nil:
        return []
    out := []
    for name in free_names(names, body):
        if not ((enclosing ! lookup(name)) is nil):
            out ! append(name)
    return out

# Which of this frame's names live in a cell. Two sources: names this body
# declares that a nested scope captures and assigns (`cell_names`), and
# captures that were already cells in the enclosing frame - the P slot
# holds that same box, so reads and writes here go through it too.
fn _cells(names: list, body, captures: list, enclosing) -> list:
    cells := cell_names(names, body)
    if not (enclosing is nil):
        for name in captures:
            if name in enclosing.cells:
                note_name(cells, name)
    return cells

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the two-pass prologue: slots first, then the cell-seeding code

# Give every name in the frame its L slot, before a line is emitted.
#
# A parameter that needs a cell gets a local slot of its own to hold the
# cell - the P slot still carries the incoming value, and the local shadows
# it from then on. The frame's own level is declared here; each nested
# block scope gets its own slots too (`allocate_block_scopes`), so an inner
# `var` shadows an outer one for the block's duration instead of writing
# through to it.
fn _allocate_slots(frame: FnContext, names: list, captures: list, body) -> nil | error:
    for name in names:
        if name in frame.cells:
            d := frame ! declare_local(name)
            if d is error:
                return d
    if len(frame.cells) > 0:
        z := frame ! reserve_zero()
        if z is error:
            return z
    own := own_declared_names(body)
    for name2 in own:
        d2 := frame ! declare_local(name2)
        if d2 is error:
            return d2
    visible := []
    for p in names:
        note_name(visible, p)
    for c in captures:
        note_name(visible, c)
    for o in own:
        note_name(visible, o)
    return frame ! allocate_block_scopes(body, visible)

# `names` minus `captures`, as a `list` (deviation 3): the cells this frame
# itself must create, excluding a captured cell that arrived already boxed.
fn _cells_to_seed(frame: FnContext, captures: list) -> list:
    out := []
    for name in frame.cells:
        if not (name in captures):
            out ! append(name)
    return out

# A small insertion sort over `str(sym)` spellings (deviation 4). Each
# frame's cell set is a handful of names, so this costs nothing.
fn _sort_names(names: list) -> list:
    out := []
    for name in names:
        text := str(name)
        out ! append(name)
        i := len(out) - 1
        while (i > 0) and (str(out[i - 1]) > text):
            tmp := out[i - 1]
            out[i - 1] = out[i]
            out[i] = tmp
            i = i - 1
    return out

# Set up the frame's own cells at entry.
#
# Every cell this frame owns is created here rather than at its
# declaration, so that every write to a cell variable is a plain `setidx`
# with no "has the box been made yet?" case anywhere in the lowering. A
# *captured* cell is not this frame's to create: it arrives in a P slot
# already holding the box the enclosing frame made.
fn _prologue(frame: FnContext, names: list, captures: list) -> nil | error:
    if len(frame.cells) == 0:
        return nil
    z := frame ! zero()
    if z is error:
        return z
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'i8, z, 0)
    if e is error:
        return e
    to_seed := _sort_names(_cells_to_seed(frame, captures))
    for name in to_seed:
        cell := frame ! lookup(name)
        if cell is nil:
            return error("internal: cell '" + str(name) + "' has no register in " + frame.name)
        mark := frame ! mark()
        seed := frame ! push()
        if seed is error:
            return seed
        if name in names:
            # The parameter's incoming value seeds its cell; windows live
            # in the L frame, so it is copied down before being boxed.
            e2 := _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, seed, frame.params[name])
            if e2 is error:
                return e2
        else:
            e3 := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=seed)
            if e3 is error:
                return e3
        # A one-element pair list is the box: `getidx`/`setidx` at index 0
        # read and write it, and the VM needs no boxing opcode of its own.
        e4 := _emit_pack(frame, 'plist, a0=cell, a1=seed, f=1)
        if e4 is error:
            return e4
        fr := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fr is error:
            return fr
    return nil

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the body and its epilogue

# Every path out of a function returns, carrying the body's value.
#
# A body whose last statement is already a `return` needs no epilogue.
# Otherwise the function's value is that last statement's - which is what
# the interpreter answers too - so the body gets a result register reserved
# below its temps and the epilogue returns it.
fn _body_and_epilogue(body, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    stmts := body_statements(body)
    n := len(stmts)
    if (n > 0) and (node_kind(stmts[n - 1]) == 'return):
        return compile_body(body, frame)
    result := frame ! push()
    if result is error:
        return result
    cb := compile_body(body, frame, result)
    if cb is error:
        return cb
    return frame ! emit_return(result)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# compile-or-stub (deviation 2)

fn _try_compile(frame: FnContext, names: list, captures: list, body) -> nil | error:
    frame.top_body = body
    a := _allocate_slots(frame, names, captures, body)
    if a is error:
        return a
    p := _prologue(frame, names, captures)
    if p is error:
        return p
    b := _body_and_epilogue(body, frame)
    if b is error:
        return b
    return frame ! patch_labels()

# Replace whatever was emitted with a single `trap`.
#
# Everything the abandoned attempt left behind is dropped with it - the
# labels it never patched, and above all its referenced-name set, which
# would otherwise have the VM resolve names the body no longer reads.
fn _make_stub(frame: FnContext) -> nil:
    (frame.code) ! resize(0)
    frame.lines = {}
    (frame.labels) ! resize(0)
    (frame.uses) ! resize(0)
    (frame.loops) ! resize(0)
    frame.top = frame.nnamed
    frame.line = nil
    words := pack('trap, f=0)
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

# Compile the body, or leave a stub that traps if it will not lower.
#
# Some functions are templates, never called - refusing the whole module
# over one of those would make it uncompilable for code that never runs,
# while compiling it as if it were callable would be worse. So the
# function keeps its table entry and its body becomes a `trap`, which is
# exactly what calling it is: unreachable code reached.
#
# Two independent knobs decide that outcome (epic 10a M0):
#   - `frame.tolerant` - set on a definition under a `@template` marking.
#     A template's code may never be run, so it stubs even in strict mode,
#     with the lowering refusal recorded as the reason.
#   - `module.stub_unlowered` - the module-wide default, false (strict)
#     since 10a M0: a lowering failure outside a template is a compile
#     error, which is what keeps the confusion stub-everything hid from
#     coming back.
# The reason is recorded on the image either way; nothing is swallowed
# silently.
fn _compile_or_stub(frame: FnContext, names: list, captures: list, body, module) -> str | nil | error:
    outcome := _try_compile(frame, names, captures, body)
    if outcome is error:
        if frame.tolerant or module.stub_unlowered:
            _make_stub(frame)
            return str(outcome)
        return outcome
    return nil

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the shared entry point: any parameter list + body -> a function table entry

# Compile any parameter list + body into a function table entry.
#
# Shared by `fn` definitions, lambdas, `defer` blocks and (M5's) class
# methods - all of them a body that runs in a frame of its own over a P
# frame of `this` values, parameters and captures, in that order (spec 1).
fn compile_callable(module, name: str, params, body, pos, flags: int = 0, enclosing = nil, dispatch: list | nil = nil, this_class = nil, tolerant: bool = false) -> list | error:
    spec := param_spec(params, module)
    if spec is error:
        return spec
    names := spec[0]
    entries := spec[1]
    param_flags := spec[2]
    f := flags | param_flags
    dispatch_list := dispatch
    if dispatch_list is nil:
        dispatch_list = []
    if len(names) > 128:
        return error(name + " declares " + str(len(names)) + " parameters, over the 128-parameter limit")
    captures := _captures(names, body, enclosing)
    frame := FnContext(module, name, names, captures, len(dispatch_list), false, this_class)
    frame.cells = _cells(names, body, captures, enclosing)
    frame.is_coroutine = (f & FN_COROUTINE) != 0
    frame.tolerant = tolerant
    ds := _declare_statics(frame, name, body, module)
    if ds is error:
        return ds
    # Positional, not keyword, arguments: pypoc's own bytecode compiler
    # (the one still compiling this file's *own* source, until this port
    # replaces it) does not support a keyword argument at a message-send
    # call site (`obj ! method(k=v)`) - confirmed by this call alone
    # tripping "the bytecode compiler does not support Kwarg yet" where an
    # otherwise-identical plain-call kwarg (`pack(op, f=f, ...)`,
    # throughout expressions.wy/statements.wy) compiles fine. A real gap in
    # the retiring compiler, not this port's to fix; noted in the epic
    # report. `add_function`'s own parameter order (context.wy) is name,
    # params, nlocals, code_offset, flags, dispatch, ncaptures, nresults,
    # uses - positional through `ncaptures` covers everything this call
    # needs, leaving `nresults`/`uses` at their defaults.
    index := (module.image) ! add_function(name, entries, 0, 0, f, dispatch_list, len(captures))
    if index is error:
        return index
    reason := _compile_or_stub(frame, names, captures, body, module)
    if reason is error:
        return reason
    entry := module.image.functions[index]
    if not (reason is nil):
        # The third field says why the stub is allowed to exist: a
        # `@template` definition (frame.tolerant) stubs even in strict
        # mode, and compiler_main's report - and the self-compile scripts'
        # stub assertion - key on that distinction.
        (module.image.unlowered) ! append([name, reason, frame.tolerant])
    entry["nlocals"] = frame.high
    entry["nresults"] = frame.max_results
    entry["uses"] = _copy_list(frame.uses)
    module ! add_pending(index, frame)
    return [index, captures]

# `static x: T = e` in a function body is a module global. It is
# module-lifetime, bound once where its owner is created rather than on
# every call (spec 7.2), so the slot is allotted here and the initializer
# is queued for module init.
fn _declare_statics(frame: FnContext, name: str, body, module) -> nil | error:
    for node in walk_body(body):
        if node_kind(node) != 'static:
            continue
        static_name := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
        init := (_field(node, 3) catch nil)
        index := module ! declare_global(name + "::" + str(static_name))
        if index is error:
            return index
        frame.statics[static_name] = index
        if not (init is nil):
            (module.pending_statics) ! append([index, init, nil])
    return nil

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# closures

# Emit a `closure` over function `index`, copying `captures` into it.
#
# The captured registers have to be contiguous, so they are copied into a
# fresh window first. A captured *cell* is copied as the cell - that is the
# whole point of the box: both frames end up pointing at the same one.
fn emit_closure(frame: FnContext, index: int, captures: list, dst) -> nil | error:
    if len(captures) == 0:
        return _emit_pack(frame, 'closure, a0=dst, a1=index, a2=0, f=0)
    base := frame ! mark()
    for name in captures:
        cap_slot := frame ! push()
        if cap_slot is error:
            return cap_slot
        reg := frame ! lookup(name)
        e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, cap_slot, reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'closure, a0=dst, a1=index, a2=L(base), f=len(captures))
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return frame ! free_to(base)

# Compile a nested `fn`/lambda/`co_lambda`/`co_def` in place, leaving the
# closure in `dst`. `boundary_params`/`boundary_body` (analysis.wy) already
# know each of those kinds' field layout, including `co_lambda`'s
# `co_params` wrapper, so this works unmodified for all four.
fn compile_closure_expr(node, frame: FnContext, dst, name, tolerant: bool = false) -> int | error:
    params := boundary_params(node)
    body := boundary_body(node)
    f := 0
    if node_kind(node) == 'co_lambda:
        f = FN_COROUTINE
    result := compile_callable(frame.module, str(name), params, body, nil, f, frame, [], nil, tolerant)
    if result is error:
        return result
    index := result[0]
    captures := result[1]
    e := emit_closure(frame, index, captures, dst)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return dst

# A nested `fn name(...)`/`co name(...)` statement: build the closure, bind
# the name. `tolerant` is a template marking handed down by statements.wy's
# `'template` handler (epic 10a M0); an ordinary nested definition defaults
# to strict.
fn compile_nested_fn(node, frame: FnContext, pos = nil, tolerant: bool = false) -> nil | error:
    mark := frame ! mark()
    reg := frame ! push()
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    name := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    e := compile_closure_expr(node, frame, reg, name, tolerant)
    if e is error:
        return e
    e2 := store_reg(name, reg, frame)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

# A `defer:` block is a zero-parameter closure armed for this frame.
#
# v1 arms at frame granularity - the block runs when the function returns,
# a deliberate narrowing of the language spec's "containing block" (spec
# 7.2, Appendix C). `defer`'s field 1 is its optional `on <type>`
# constraint; this port only distinguishes "no constraint" (mode 0, always)
# from "some constraint" (mode 1, on error) - pypoc's own `Defer.on_error`
# is the same plain bool, so `defer_reg`'s mode-2 ("on error or nil",
# doc/wyc-format.md 6.3) is a POC-vs-spec gap this epic does not close
# either (out of scope per the epic's own "already documented" carve-out).
fn compile_defer(node, frame: FnContext) -> nil | error:
    constraint := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    body := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    result := compile_callable(frame.module, "<defer>", [], body, nil, 0, frame, [], nil)
    if result is error:
        return result
    index := result[0]
    captures := result[1]
    mark := frame ! mark()
    reg := frame ! push()
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := emit_closure(frame, index, captures, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    mode := 0
    if not (constraint is nil):
        mode = 1
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'defer_reg, a0=reg, f=mode)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return frame ! free_to(mark)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# close the cycle-breaking hooks (deviation 7): expressions.wy's `_lambda`
# and statements.wy's `_nested_fn`/`_defer` were left as one-shot hooks for
# exactly this file to install, the same way statements.wy closes
# expressions.wy's `_do` hook at its own bottom.
set_closure_compiler(compile_closure_expr)
set_nested_fn_compiler(compile_nested_fn)
set_defer_compiler(compile_defer)
